Component scanning is a Spring feature that automatically detects and registers beans from specified base packages. It looks for classes annotated with stereotype annotations like `@Component`, `@Service`, `@Repository`, and `@Controller`. This reduces the need for explicit bean declarations.

```java
@Configuration
@ComponentScan(basePackages = "com.example")
public class AppConfig {
    // Configuration
}
```

Constructor injection is generally preferred because:

- It ensures all required dependencies are provided at initialization time
- It promotes immutability (fields can be final)
- It makes dependencies explicit and visible
- It makes testing easier, as dependencies can be mocked in unit tests
- It prevents circular dependencies at compile time

```java
@Service
public class UserServiceImpl implements UserService {
    private final UserRepository userRepository;
    private final EmailService emailService;

    public UserServiceImpl(UserRepository userRepository, EmailService emailService) {
        this.userRepository = userRepository;
        this.emailService = emailService;
    }

    // Methods
}
```

Spring resolves property placeholders (like `${property.name}`) using:

1. `PropertySourcesPlaceholderConfigurer` - processes placeholders in bean definitions
2. `@Value` annotation for injecting property values into beans
3. `Environment` interface for programmatic property access
4. Property sources like property files, environment variables, JVM system properties

```java
@Configuration
@PropertySource("classpath:application.properties")
public class AppConfig {
    @Value("${database.url}")
    private String databaseUrl;

    // Bean definitions
}
```

The `@Qualifier` annotation is used to disambiguate bean wiring when multiple beans of the same type exist in the container. It helps Spring identify exactly which bean should be injected when there's ambiguity.

For example, if you have multiple implementations of `PaymentService` interface:

```java
@Component("creditCardPayment")
public class CreditCardPaymentService implements PaymentService { ... }

@Component("paypalPayment")
public class PayPalPaymentService implements PaymentService { ... }

// In another component:
@Autowired
@Qualifier("paypalPayment")
private PaymentService paymentService; // Specifically injects PayPalPaymentService
```

Without `@Qualifier`, Spring would throw a `NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException` since it wouldn't know which implementation to inject.
